Task 1: Build and Upload an Execution Environment
Subtask 1.1: Understanding Execution Environments
Execution Environments are container images that provide a consistent, portable, and reproducible way to execute Ansible automation. They package all the dependencies, collections, and Python libraries needed for your automation tasks.

Key benefits: • Consistency: Same environment across development, testing, and production • Isolation: Dependencies don't conflict between different automation projects • Portability: Can run anywhere containers are supported • Version Control: Track and manage environment changes

Subtask 1.2: Prepare the Build Environment
First, let's set up our workspace and verify the required tools are available.

# Create a working directory for our Execution Environment
mkdir -p ~/ee-lab
cd ~/ee-lab

# Verify ansible-builder is installed
ansible-builder --version

# Verify podman is available
podman --version

# Check if we can connect to the registry
podman login --get-login registry.redhat.io
Subtask 1.3: Create Execution Environment Definition Files
Create the necessary files to define our custom Execution Environment. We'll build an environment that includes additional Python libraries and Ansible collections.

Create the execution-environment.yml file:

cat > execution-environment.yml << 'EOF'
---
version: 3

images:
  base_image:
    name: 'registry.redhat.io/ubi8/ubi:latest'

dependencies:
  galaxy: requirements.yml
  python: requirements.txt
  system: bindep.txt

additional_build_steps:
  prepend_base:
    - RUN whoami
    - RUN cat /etc/os-release
  prepend_galaxy:
    - RUN ansible-galaxy --version
  prepend_builder:
    - RUN echo "Building custom execution environment"
  append_final:
    - RUN echo "Custom EE build completed"
    - RUN ls -la /usr/share/ansible

build_arg_defaults:
  ANSIBLE_GALAXY_CLI_COLLECTION_OPTS: "--pre"
EOF
Create the requirements.yml file for Ansible collections:

cat > requirements.yml << 'EOF'
---
collections:
  - name: community.general
    version: ">=6.0.0"
  - name: ansible.posix
    version: ">=1.4.0"
  - name: community.crypto
    version: ">=2.10.0"
  - name: community.mysql
    version: ">=3.5.0"
  - name: community.postgresql
    version: ">=2.3.0"
EOF
Create the requirements.txt file for Python packages:

cat > requirements.txt << 'EOF'
requests>=2.28.0
jinja2>=3.1.0
pyyaml>=6.0
cryptography>=3.4.8
psycopg2-binary>=2.9.0
pymysql>=1.0.0
netaddr>=0.8.0
dnspython>=2.2.0
EOF
Create the bindep.txt file for system packages:

cat > bindep.txt << 'EOF'
git [platform:rpm]
rsync [platform:rpm]
openssh-clients [platform:rpm]
sshpass [platform:rpm]
curl [platform:rpm]
wget [platform:rpm]
unzip [platform:rpm]
tar [platform:rpm]
EOF
Subtask 1.4: Build the Execution Environment
Now let's build our custom Execution Environment using ansible-builder.

# Build the execution environment
ansible-builder build \
  --tag custom-ee:1.0 \
  --container-runtime podman \
  --verbosity 2 \
  .

# Verify the image was created
podman images | grep custom-ee

# Inspect the image details
podman inspect custom-ee:1.0 | head -20
Subtask 1.5: Test the Execution Environment Locally
Before uploading, let's test our Execution Environment locally to ensure it works correctly.

Create a test playbook:

cat > test-playbook.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Test Custom Execution Environment
  hosts: localhost
  gather_facts: yes
  tasks:
    - name: Display Ansible version
      debug:
        msg: "Ansible version: {{ ansible_version.full }}"

    - name: Test community.general collection
      community.general.system_info:
      register: system_info

    - name: Display system information
      debug:
        msg: "OS Family: {{ system_info.system_info.distribution }}"

    - name: Test Python requests library
      uri:
        url: "https://httpbin.org/get"
        method: GET
      register: http_test

    - name: Display HTTP test result
      debug:
        msg: "HTTP test status: {{ http_test.status }}"

    - name: Test crypto functionality
      community.crypto.random_string:
        length: 16
      register: random_string

    - name: Display random string
      debug:
        msg: "Generated random string: {{ random_string.random_string }}"
EOF
Run the test playbook using our custom EE:

# Test the execution environment
podman run --rm -it \
  -v $(pwd):/tmp/work:Z \
  -w /tmp/work \
  custom-ee:1.0 \
  ansible-playbook test-playbook.yml

# Check if all tasks completed successfully
echo "Exit code: $?"
